<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <title>@ViewData["Title"] · HTMLX Pages</title>
    @* The brand's stylesheets, in the order the brand gives: tokens first, feedback last. *@
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="~/css/tokens.css" />
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="~/css/base.css" />
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="~/css/layout.css" />
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="~/css/navigation.css" />
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="~/css/data-display.css" />
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="~/css/inputs.css" />
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="~/css/feedback.css" />
    @*
        HTMX 4.0.1, the one version this application is built and tested with; README.md records it. The scripts sit in
        the head because HTMX 4 answers the back button by swapping a fresh page body in: a script in the body would
        run again, and load a second HTMX.
    *@
    <script src="~/js/htmx.min.js" defer></script>
    <script src="~/js/app.js" defer></script>
</head>
@*
    Every link and form in the application loads over HTMX: a link to another page of it fetches that page and swaps
    its body into this one, keeps the address bar and Back in step, and takes the new page's title. HTMX leaves alone a
    link to another site, one that opens a new tab, one to a part of this page (#main) and a form that says
    method="dialog". A form that posts says hx-post, which HTMX sets up before boosting, so it keeps its own target. A
    page that HTMX loaded puts the keyboard on its heading, as each page model says, since HTMX moves it nowhere.
    Without HTMX every link and form works as it always did.
*@
<body hx-boost:inherited="true">
    <a class="skip-link" href="#main">Skip to content</a>
    <div class="app">
        <header class="app-header">
            <button class="btn ghost icon-only" type="button" data-action="toggle-nav" aria-label="Main menu" aria-controls="app-nav">
                <svg class="icon" aria-hidden="true"><use href="/icons.svg#menu"></use></svg>
            </button>
            <a class="brand" asp-page="/Index">
                <svg class="brand-mark" aria-hidden="true"><use href="/icons.svg#logo"></use></svg>
                HTMLX Pages
            </a>
            <div class="end">
                <span class="avatar" role="img" aria-label="Signed in as Demo User">DU</span>
            </div>
        </header>

        @* Each page names its section in ViewData, and the section's link is marked as the current page. The foot of
           the nav holds the one link that leaves the application, to its source on GitHub, which opens a new tab. *@
        <nav class="app-nav" id="app-nav" aria-label="Main">
            <a asp-page="/Organizations/Index" title="Organizations"
               aria-current="@(ViewData["Section"] as string == "Organizations" ? "page" : null)">
                <svg class="icon" aria-hidden="true"><use href="/icons.svg#building"></use></svg><span>Organizations</span>
            </a>
            <a asp-page="/Users/Index" title="Users"
               aria-current="@(ViewData["Section"] as string == "Users" ? "page" : null)">
                <svg class="icon" aria-hidden="true"><use href="/icons.svg#users"></use></svg><span>Users</span>
            </a>
            <a asp-page="/Components/Index" title="Components"
               aria-current="@(ViewData["Section"] as string == "Components" ? "page" : null)">
                <svg class="icon" aria-hidden="true"><use href="/icons.svg#palette"></use></svg><span>Components</span>
            </a>
            <div class="end">
                <a href="https://github.com/mattonweb/Razor-Pages-With-HTMX" target="_blank" rel="noopener"
                   title="GitHub (opens in a new tab)">
                    <svg class="icon" aria-hidden="true"><use href="/icons.svg#external"></use></svg><span>GitHub</span><span class="visually-hidden"> (opens in a new tab)</span>
                </a>
            </div>
        </nav>

        <main class="app-main" id="main">
            @RenderBody()
        </main>
    </div>

    @*
        The one region toasts appear in, as the brand asks. It is empty unless a page puts a toast in its Toasts section;
        after an action HTMX sent, the response replaces what it holds. Being a live region from the start, it has
        screen readers announce each toast as it arrives.
    *@
    <div class="toasts" id="toasts" role="status" aria-live="polite">@await RenderSectionAsync("Toasts", required: false)</div>

    @*
        What the application's script puts in the toast region when a request HTMX sent fails, in place of swapping
        anything: an answer the page did not plan for, no answer before HTMX gives up waiting, or none at all. The
        toasts are written here, in the brand's markup, so that the script holds no words of its own. A template's
        content is not part of the page until the script copies it out, and without script it never is.
    *@
    @{
        Toast unplannedAnswer = new Toast
        {
            Message = "Something went wrong, so that may not have been done. Reload the page, then try again.",
        };
        Toast timedOut = new Toast
        {
            Message = "The server took too long to answer, so that may not have been done. "
                + "Reload the page, then try again.",
        };
        Toast connectionLost = new Toast
        {
            Message = "The page couldn't reach the server. Check your connection, then try again.",
        };
    }
    <template id="request-failed-answer"><partial name="_Toast" model="unplannedAnswer" /></template>
    <template id="request-failed-timeout"><partial name="_Toast" model="timedOut" /></template>
    <template id="request-failed-connection"><partial name="_Toast" model="connectionLost" /></template>
    @await RenderSectionAsync("Scripts", required: false)
</body>
</html>
